<Page
	title="Now"
	description="What I'm studying, building, and asking right now."
	dateline="Last updated August 2026"
>
	<div class="prose">
		<h2>Studying</h2>
		<p>
			Yergin's <em>The New Map</em>, which keeps rearranging how I think about energy security.
			The USCC's annual reports and its evaluation of Made in China 2025, drier on the cover
			than they are inside. And the current generation of quantum hardware from IBM Quantum,
			where I work, because keeping up with the machines is the only way to keep the rest of my
			quantum writing honest.
		</p>
		<p>
			Classes at NDU start this month, so this list is about to change.
		</p>

		<h2>Building</h2>
		<p>
			This site, for one. And a set of tools built on public data with help from IBM Bob: a
			Defense Industrial Base
			Atlas, which is the furthest along, and a European Energy Dashboard. Both are attempts to
			see industrial capacity plainly: who can make what, where, and how fast.
		</p>
		<p>
			Writing here weekly, starting now. If a week gets away from me, I'd rather post a short
			note than nothing.
		</p>

		<h2>Asking</h2>
		<p>
			Whether the data we already collect can be made to answer new questions. The US has
			world-class economic statistics. But the numbers sit in many places with no way to see
			them together, and a question like who makes a given part, and where, falls between the
			datasets. This is my exercise in learning to work with public industrial data and make it
			simpler to use for the people designing industrial policy.
		</p>
	</div>
</Page>
